---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const updated = 'September 2026';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Booth Rent vs Commission: The Real Math for Stylists (2026) — Main Street Toolbox"
  description="Booth rent vs commission compared: typical splits (you keep 50–60%), booth rent ranges, the 15–25% rent guideline, and the exact sales number where renting starts winning."
  path="/guides/booth-rent-vs-commission/"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Career math · {updated}</p>
    <h1>Booth rent vs commission: which pays you more?</h1>
    <p><b>The short answer:</b> with a typical 55% commission split, booth rent beats commission once your
    service sales pass roughly <b>rent ÷ 0.45 per month</b> — about $2,700/month in sales for a $1,200 booth.
    Below that line, commission pays more. The catch is everything the line doesn't show: supplies, taxes, and
    slow weeks.</p>

    <h2>The two structures, honestly</h2>
    <p><b>Commission:</b> the salon keeps a split (typically 40–50%, so you keep 50–60%) and covers rent,
    water, electricity, front desk, product and laundry. Your money is smooth — a slow week is a smaller
    paycheck, not a loss.</p>
    <p><b>Booth rent:</b> a flat weekly or monthly fee ($400–1,200+/mo depending on market and salon), and you
    keep every dollar of service revenue. You also buy your own color and product, do your own laundry and
    books, and pay the rent in a slow week all the same.</p>

    <h2>The 15–25% rule</h2>
    <p>The working guideline: <b>rent should run 15–25% of your service revenue.</b> A $1,200 booth needs
    roughly $5,000–8,000 in monthly service sales to stay inside it. Above 25%, only a very loyal rebooked
    book makes renting work — negotiate the rent or keep building on commission first.</p>

    <h2>Your break-even line</h2>
    <p>Commission nets you <b>sales × your split</b>. Renting nets you <b>sales − rent</b>. Setting them equal
    gives the crossover: <b>sales* = rent ÷ (1 − your split)</b>.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Booth rent</th><th>Crossover at 55% split</th><th>Crossover at 60% split</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>$800/mo</td><td>$1,778/mo sales (~$410/wk)</td><td>$2,000/mo sales (~$460/wk)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,000/mo</td><td>$2,222/mo sales (~$515/wk)</td><td>$2,500/mo sales (~$580/wk)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,200/mo</td><td>$2,667/mo sales (~$615/wk)</td><td>$3,000/mo sales (~$695/wk)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Pass the line consistently for three months before you switch — one strong month is a schedule
    anomaly, not a book.</p>

    <h2>What the math leaves out</h2>
    <p><b>Self-employment tax and benefits</b> (both structures are usually IC now, but renters feel it
    first), <b>product and supply costs</b> (color alone can run 5–8% of service sales), <b>no-paycheck
    weeks</b>, and <b>the rebook rate</b> — a book that's 80%+ rebooked is the real qualification for going
    independent. Renting with a walk-in-dependent book is renting risk.</p>

    <h2>Run it with your numbers</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/salon/booth-rent-commission-calculator/">Booth Rent vs Commission Calculator</a> compares
    both paths from your weekly sales, your split and the rent quote — including the 15–25% check. When you
    have a price list of your own, build it with the
    <a href="/salon/service-price-list-builder/">Service Price List Builder</a>, and take payments with a clean
    <a href="/salon/receipt-generator/">Receipt Generator</a>.</p>

    <h2>Questions stylists ask</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>How much is booth rent on average?</summary><p>$400–1,200+/month depending on market, salon and whether product is included. Judge any quote against the 15–25%-of-revenue guideline, not against other stylists' rents.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What commission split should a new stylist expect?</summary><p>New stylists commonly start keeping 40–50%, moving toward 60% with a proven book. Splits above 60% usually come with fewer salon-provided supports — compare the whole package, not the split.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Do tips change the comparison?</summary><p>No — tips follow you under both structures. Keep the comparison to service revenue, and keep tips out of the qualification math entirely.</p></details>
      <details><summary>When should I NOT rent even if the math works?</summary><p>When your book isn't rebooked. Renting converts a stable paycheck into a business with fixed costs; an 80%+ rebook rate is the practical threshold most owners quote.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
